
  THE BERNOULLI-SYLVESTER CHAIN THROUGH 42

======================================================================

  I. VON STAUDT-CLAUSEN AND THE BERNOULLI SIEVE
  --------------------------------------------------

  denom(B_{2k}) = prod{p prime : (p-1)|2k}

    2k         denom  primes
  ----         -----  ------
     2             6  [2, 3]
     4            30  [2, 3, 5]
     6            42  [2, 3, 7]
     8            30  [2, 3, 5]
    10            66  [2, 3, 11]
    12          2730  [2, 3, 5, 7, 13]
    14             6  [2, 3]
    16           510  [2, 3, 5, 17]
    18           798  [2, 3, 7, 19]
    20           330  [2, 3, 5, 11]
    22           138  [2, 3, 23]
    24          2730  [2, 3, 5, 7, 13]
    26             6  [2, 3]
    28           870  [2, 3, 5, 29]
    30         14322  [2, 3, 7, 11, 31]
    32           510  [2, 3, 5, 17]
    34             6  [2, 3]
    36       1919190  [2, 3, 5, 7, 13, 19, 37]
    38             6  [2, 3]
    40         13530  [2, 3, 5, 11, 41]
    42          1806  [2, 3, 7, 43]
    44           690  [2, 3, 5, 23]
    46           282  [2, 3, 47]
    48         46410  [2, 3, 5, 7, 13, 17]

  II. THE SYLVESTER SEQUENCE
  --------------------------------------------------

  a_1 = 2 (PRIME)
  a_2 = 3 (PRIME)
  a_3 = 7 (PRIME)
  a_4 = 43 (PRIME)
  a_5 = 1807 (composite)
  a_6 = 3263443 (PRIME)
  a_7 = 10650056950807 ((too large for trial division))

  Partial products P_k = a_1 * ... * a_k:
  P_1 = 2
  P_2 = 6
  P_3 = 42
  P_4 = 1806
  P_5 = 3263442

  III. THE CHAIN: B_6 -> 42 -> B_42 -> 1806 -> B_1806 -> ...
  --------------------------------------------------

  Step 0: B_{6} has denom = 42
    Primes: [2, 3, 7]

  Step 1: B_{42} has denom = 1806
    Primes: [2, 3, 7, 43]

  Step 2: B_{1806} has denom = 1806
    Primes: [2, 3, 7, 43]

  Step 3: B_{1806} has denom = 1806
    Primes: [2, 3, 7, 43]


  IV. VON STAUDT MEETS SYLVESTER
  --------------------------------------------------

  P_3 = 2*3*7 = 42 = denom(B_6)
  P_4 = 2*3*7*43 = 1806 = denom(B_42)

  The chain: denom(B_{P_k}) =? P_{k+1}

  1806 = 2*3*7*43 has 16 divisors
  VS primes for B_1806: [2, 3, 7, 43]
  denom(B_1806) = 1806

  denom(B_1806) = 2 * 3 * 7 * 43

  P_5 = 2*3*7*43*1807 = 3263442
  But 1807 = 13*139 is COMPOSITE.
  So P_5 != denom(B_1806).

  Does 13 appear? (p-1)=12 | 1806? False. 13 NOT in denom
  Does 139 appear? (p-1)=138 | 1806? False. 139 NOT in denom

  Extra primes (beyond Sylvester): []

  CONCLUSION: The VS chain and Sylvester chain agree for steps 1-4:
  denom(B_{P_k}) = P_{k+1} when a_{k+2} is prime.
  They DIVERGE at step 5 because a_5 = 1807 is composite.
  denom(B_1806) CONTAINS 13*139 = 1807 but also other primes.

  V. THE FORMAL GROUP LOG AND BERNOULLI
  --------------------------------------------------

  arctanh(x) = x + x^3/3 + x^5/5 + x^7/7 + ...

  Prime-degree coefficients (1/p poles):
    x^ 3: coeff 1/3. p=3 appears in denom(B_{2}) (since (3-1)=2 | 2)
    x^ 5: coeff 1/5. p=5 appears in denom(B_{4}) (since (5-1)=4 | 4)
    x^ 7: coeff 1/7. p=7 appears in denom(B_{6}) (since (7-1)=6 | 6)
    x^11: coeff 1/11. p=11 appears in denom(B_{10}) (since (11-1)=10 | 10)
    x^13: coeff 1/13. p=13 appears in denom(B_{12}) (since (13-1)=12 | 12)
    x^17: coeff 1/17. p=17 appears in denom(B_{16}) (since (17-1)=16 | 16)
    x^19: coeff 1/19. p=19 appears in denom(B_{18}) (since (19-1)=18 | 18)
    x^23: coeff 1/23. p=23 appears in denom(B_{22}) (since (23-1)=22 | 22)
    x^29: coeff 1/29. p=29 appears in denom(B_{28}) (since (29-1)=28 | 28)

  The primes in denom(B_6) = {2,3,7} come from:
  - p=2: degree 2 would be x^2, but arctanh has no even terms!
    Instead: 2 enters via (2-1)=1 | 6.
  - p=3: x^3/3 pole at degree 3. (3-1)=2 | 6.
  - p=7: x^7/7 pole at degree 7. (7-1)=6 | 6.
  - p=5: x^5/5 pole at degree 5. But (5-1)=4 does NOT divide 6.
    So 5 is excluded from denom(B_6)! The arctanh has the pole
    but the divisibility condition filters it out.

  The sieve: arctanh provides ALL primes as potential poles,
  but (p-1)|2k filters to the subset entering denom(B_{2k}).

  VI. SYNTHESIS
  --------------------------------------------------

  Three structures converge at 42:

  1. BERNOULLI (von Staudt-Clausen):
     B_6 = 1/42 because {2,3,7} are the primes with (p-1)|6.
     B_42 has denom 1806 = 42*43 because 43 is prime and 42|42.
     Chain: 6 -> 42 -> 1806 -> ... (denom of B_{2k} feeds next 2k)

  2. SYLVESTER (Egyptian fractions):
     2, 3, 7, 43, 1807=13*139, ...
     Partial products: 2, 6, 42, 1806, 3263442, ...
     Agrees with Bernoulli chain through 1806 (4 steps).
     Diverges when Sylvester term is composite (1807).

  3. CAYLEY (formal group):
     log_F = arctanh has 1/p at prime degrees.
     These poles are the 'raw material' for Bernoulli denominators.
     The (p-1)|2k sieve selects which poles contribute.

  4. UNIQUENESS:
     42 is the unique squarefree n with sigma(n) = 2n + phi(n).
     abundancy(42) - 2 = phi(42)/42 = 2/7 = Cayley address of Q^{-1}(2)... 
     wait: Q^{-1}(2) = 1/3. 2/7 = phi(42)/42. Different.
     But: 2/7 = doubler/forbidden in the Hurwitz arithmetic.

  42 = 2*3*7 = P_3 = denom(B_6) = disc(Hurwitz)
  = unique squarefree solution to abundance = totient
  = the number where three arithmetic functions perfectly balance.

